<p class="gna-lede">A face lift (rhytidectomy) is surgery to reposition the sagging tissues of the lower face and neck — the jowls, the deepening folds beside the mouth and the loose skin under the jaw. Modern techniques lift the deeper supporting layer, called the SMAS, rather than simply pulling the skin, which tends to give a more natural and longer-lasting result. Many people feel presentable within two to three weeks, with the final result settling over several months, but a face lift is major surgery with real risks, and it does not stop ageing.</p>

<h2 id="what-a-face-lift-does">What does a face lift actually do?</h2>
<p>With age, the cheek tissues descend, the ligaments that anchor the face loosen, fat shifts and the skin loses elasticity. The result is jowling along the jawline, deeper nasolabial folds and marionette lines, and loose skin or vertical bands in the neck.</p>
<p>Beneath the skin and fat lies a layer of fibrous tissue and muscle called the <strong>superficial musculoaponeurotic system (SMAS)</strong>, which is continuous with the platysma muscle of the neck. Almost all modern face lifts work on this layer: the surgeon lifts and repositions the SMAS, then redrapes the skin over it without tension and removes the excess. Supporting the deeper layer, rather than tightening the skin alone, is what helps avoid a "pulled" or "wind-swept" look.</p>
<p>A face lift mainly improves the <strong>lower two-thirds of the face and the neck</strong>. It does not change fine wrinkles or skin texture, lift the brows or remove eyelid hooding — those are addressed by other treatments.</p>

<div class="gna-keypoints">
  <h3>Key points</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>A face lift repositions the deeper SMAS layer as well as the skin, improving jowls, folds and the neck.</li>
    <li>Techniques range from limited "mini" lifts to more extensive SMAS and deep-plane lifts; each has trade-offs.</li>
    <li>Haematoma (a collection of blood) is the most common significant early complication; nerve injury is uncommon and usually temporary.</li>
    <li>Smoking and other nicotine use greatly increase the risk of skin healing problems.</li>
    <li>Expect two to three weeks before you feel presentable and several months for the result to settle; results vary.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2 id="techniques">Types of face lift</h2>
<p>Surgeons use different names for similar operations, which can be confusing. The main differences are how long the incision is and how deep and wide the dissection goes. No single technique suits everyone, and studies comparing them have not shown one to be clearly superior for all patients; your anatomy and the surgeon's experience with a given technique matter most.</p>
<h3>SMAS face lift</h3>
<p>The most widely used approach. Through an incision that runs in front of and behind the ear, the skin is lifted off the SMAS, and the SMAS is then tightened — by folding and stitching it (plication), by removing a strip and closing the gap (SMASectomy), or by raising it as a separate flap and repositioning it. It suits many people with moderate jowling and neck laxity.</p>
<h3>Mini (short-scar) face lift</h3>
<p>A shorter incision, mainly around the front of the ear, with a more limited lift of the SMAS. Recovery is often quicker, but the lift is smaller, the neck improves less and the effect may not last as long. It generally suits younger patients with early jowling and good skin quality.</p>
<h3>Deep-plane face lift</h3>
<p>The surgeon works beneath the SMAS in key areas and releases the retaining ligaments that tether the cheek, then lifts the skin and SMAS together as a single layer. Supporters argue that this moves the cheek and the area beside the nose more effectively. Others point out that the dissection runs closer to branches of the facial nerve and demands specific experience. Evidence comparing long-term outcomes with SMAS techniques is mixed, and both approaches can produce natural results in experienced hands.</p>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th></th><th>Mini lift</th><th>SMAS lift</th><th>Deep-plane lift</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Incision</td><td>Shorter, mainly in front of the ear</td><td>Around the ear, into the hairline</td><td>Around the ear, into the hairline</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Depth of work</td><td>Limited SMAS tightening</td><td>SMAS tightened or repositioned</td><td>Beneath the SMAS, with ligament release</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Typical candidate</td><td>Early jowling, little neck laxity</td><td>Moderate jowling and neck laxity</td><td>Moderate to marked descent of the cheeks and jowls</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Neck improvement</td><td>Limited</td><td>Good, especially with a neck lift</td><td>Good, especially with a neck lift</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Main trade-off</td><td>Quicker recovery, smaller and shorter-lived change</td><td>Versatile; well-established</td><td>Greater mobilisation; closer to nerve branches</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>You may also see "thread lifts" advertised. These use dissolvable barbed sutures placed under the skin. They give a modest, short-lived improvement and are not a substitute for a surgical face lift; visible or palpable threads, dimpling and infection can occur.</p>

<h2 id="neck-lift">Neck lift and combined procedures</h2>
<p>The neck is often what bothers people most. A <strong>neck lift</strong> may involve tightening the platysma muscle in the midline through a small incision under the chin (platysmaplasty) to soften vertical bands, removing or sculpting fat beneath the chin, and redraping neck skin through the face-lift incisions behind the ears. Because the face and neck are continuous, the two are commonly done together; an isolated neck lift suits a smaller group of patients.</p>
<p>Other procedures sometimes combined with a face lift include a brow lift, eyelid surgery, fat grafting to restore volume, chin augmentation and skin resurfacing. Combining procedures can mean one anaesthetic and one recovery, but it lengthens the operation and may increase some risks, so it is weighed individually. Our guide to <a href="/blogs/brow-lift-vs-botox-droopy-brows/">brow lift versus Botox</a> and our page on <a href="/eyelid-surgery">eyelid surgery</a> explain those options.</p>

<h2 id="candidates">Who is a suitable candidate?</h2>
<p>A face lift tends to suit people who:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Have visible jowls, deep folds or loose neck skin that genuinely bother them</li>
  <li>Are in good general health, with conditions such as high blood pressure and diabetes well controlled</li>
  <li>Do not use nicotine, or are willing to stop well before and after surgery</li>
  <li>Have a stable weight and understand what the operation can and cannot achieve</li>
</ul>
<p>For milder changes, non-surgical treatments may be more appropriate. <a href="/blogs/dermal-fillers-types-safety-what-to-expect/">Dermal fillers</a> can restore some lost volume, and botulinum toxin softens expression lines, though neither lifts sagging skin. There is no "right age" for a face lift; the decision rests on your anatomy, your health and your priorities.</p>

