<h2 id="risks">Risks you should know about</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Haematoma:</strong> a collection of blood under the skin, usually within the first 24 hours. It is the most common significant early complication and is more likely in men, in people with high blood pressure, and in those taking blood-thinning medicines or certain supplements. A large haematoma needs a prompt return to theatre to drain it. Careful blood-pressure control around surgery lowers the risk.</li>
  <li><strong>Facial nerve injury:</strong> the branches that move the face run beneath the SMAS. Temporary weakness — for example, of the lower lip or the brow — is uncommon and usually recovers over weeks to months; permanent weakness is rare.</li>
  <li><strong>Numbness:</strong> the cheeks and the skin around the ears are numb for weeks to months. Injury to the great auricular nerve can cause longer-lasting numbness of the earlobe.</li>
  <li><strong>Skin loss or poor healing:</strong> areas of skin, usually behind the ear, may heal slowly or break down. The risk is substantially higher in smokers.</li>
  <li><strong>Scarring:</strong> scars are placed in natural creases and the hairline, but some heal thicker or wider than expected.</li>
  <li><strong>Hair and earlobe changes:</strong> hair loss near the temple incisions, a shift in the hairline or sideburn, or a pulled earlobe.</li>
  <li><strong>Asymmetry, contour irregularities or an outcome different from what you hoped</strong>, which occasionally leads to revision surgery.</li>
  <li><strong>Infection, blood clots and the general risks of anaesthesia.</strong></li>
</ul>
<p>Our article on the <a href="/blogs/side-effects-of-maxillofacial-surgery-risks-complications-and-prevention/">risks and side effects of facial surgery</a> covers general surgical risks in more detail.</p>

<h2 id="smoking">Why smoking matters</h2>
<p>Nicotine narrows small blood vessels. In a face lift, the skin is lifted away from its deeper layer and depends on a reduced blood supply while it heals, so smoking substantially increases the risk of skin breakdown, wound problems and poor scars. This applies to cigarettes and bidis, to vaping and nicotine replacement products, and to chewed tobacco such as gutka and khaini, which also deliver nicotine. Surgeons commonly ask patients to stop all nicotine for at least four weeks before and after surgery, and some will not operate on people who continue to smoke.</p>

<h2 id="recovery-timeline">Recovery timeline</h2>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>When</th><th>What to expect</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Day of surgery</td><td>Surgery under general anaesthesia or sedation, often several hours depending on the procedures combined; an overnight stay is common. A head dressing and small drains may be used.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Days 1–3</td><td>Drains and bulky dressings are removed; tightness, swelling and bruising peak. Keep your head raised, rest and take short walks.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Week 1</td><td>Most stitches are removed between days five and ten. Swelling and bruising begin to settle; numbness is normal.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Weeks 2–3</td><td>Many people feel comfortable in public and return to desk work, sometimes using make-up over fading bruises.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Weeks 4–6</td><td>Gradual return to exercise and most normal activities, as advised by your surgeon.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Months 3–6</td><td>Most residual swelling and firmness resolve; sensation gradually returns.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Months 6–12</td><td>Scars mature and fade, and the final result becomes clear.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>These timings are typical, not fixed. Recovery varies with your age, health, the extent of surgery and how closely aftercare instructions are followed. Report sudden swelling on one side, increasing pain, fever or wound discharge to your surgical team straight away.</p>

<h2 id="expectations">Realistic expectations and when to see a specialist</h2>
<p>A well-planned face lift can make the jawline and neck look firmer and the face look more rested. The aim is a refreshed version of you, not a different person. It cannot halt ageing: the face continues to change after surgery, and how long the improvement remains noticeable varies widely. Skin quality, sun damage, weight changes and genetics all influence the outcome.</p>
<p>Consider seeing a specialist if sagging in the lower face or neck bothers you and non-surgical treatments have not given the change you want. A good consultation should include a full medical assessment, an explanation of all options — including non-surgical ones and choosing to do nothing — a frank discussion of risks and recovery, and time to decide without pressure.</p>
<p>At Gnathos Facial in Gachibowli, Hyderabad, face lift surgery is planned only after an in-person examination and is carried out at NABH-accredited partner hospitals. Cosmetic face lifts are generally not covered by health insurance. Learn more about <a href="/face-lift-surgery">face lift surgery at Gnathos Facial</a>, or <a href="/book-appointment">book a consultation</a> to discuss your options.</p>
